# G066 — Download Manager

*v2. See `download-manager.md` for prose: queue as rendezvous, pull
scheduling for emergent load balancing, worker count as resource
cap, fan-out + fan-in (map-reduce shape), per-job transactions for
failure isolation, batch-scope cancellation.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Parallel Vault Reindex

[reindex-vault @worker-count
    IN ORDER TO
        enqueue every vault note for re-parse and upsert
        and run a worker pool that isolates per-note failures
        so one malformed note doesn't abort the whole reindex

    @notes <- [vault[all-notes]]
    @mgr   <- [download[manager {worker-count: @worker-count}]]

    for @note in @notes
        [download[enqueue
                    {manager: @mgr,
                     job: {url: "file://" + @note:path,
                            expected-bytes: @note:size}}]]

    @results <- [download[run-with
                            {manager: @mgr,
                             simulate: [note[reparse-and-upsert]]}]]

    @ok     <- @results where status is "completed"
    @failed <- @results where status is "failed"

    [log[write
          {level: "info",
           message: "reindex: " + count(@ok) + " ok, " +
                     count(@failed) + " failed"}]]

    for @r in @failed
        [log[write
              {level: "warn",
               url: @r:url, error: @r:error}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[download
    manager         {worker-count}
    enqueue         {manager, job}
    run-with        {manager, simulate}            ;; -> [Result]
    cancel          {manager}                       ;; batch-scoped
    queue-length    {manager}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: pull schedules balance `where` worker is idle

[download[run-with {manager: @m, simulate: @sim}]
    IN ORDER TO
        let every worker pull its next job whenever it finishes the current
        so slow jobs do not block idle workers and load balances emergently

    spawn @m:worker-count workers, each running:
        loop
            @job <- [queue[pop {queue: @m:queue}]]
                || -> break (queue empty and closed)
            @m
                where is-cancelled is true -> emit Cancelled outcome, break
            @out <- [@sim {job: @job}]
                catching any resistance -> emit Failed outcome with reason
            append @out to shared results (protected by results-lock)
    join all workers
    return results
]

Idle workers take work; busy workers don't. No dispatcher guesses
cost. Push scheduling requires predicting job size and worker load;
pull punts the whole problem to workers and wins.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: per-job transaction `where` failure is isolated

[worker loop
    @job wrapped in try/catch
        where simulate succeeds  -> emit Completed {outcome}
        where simulate resists   -> emit Failed {reason} — continue to next job
        where @m:is-cancelled    -> emit Cancelled — break loop
]

Partial failure is a first-class outcome co-equal with success.
G065's cancellation was fatal; G066 promotes failure to per-job,
making "9997 of 10000 succeeded" the natural reporting shape.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @mgr <- [download[manager {worker-count: 4}]]
    for @i in [1..16]
        [download[enqueue
                    {manager: @mgr,
                     job: {url: "http://example/" + @i}}]]

    @results <- [download[run-with
                            {manager: @mgr,
                             simulate: @fake-download}]]

    -> count(@results)                               ;; -> 16
    -> @results where status is "completed" count    ;; -> 16 (happy path)

    @mgr2 <- [download[manager {worker-count: 4}]]
    for @i in [1..100]
        [download[enqueue {manager: @mgr2, job: {url: "..."}}]]
    concurrent [
        @r <- [download[run-with
                          {manager: @mgr2, simulate: @slow-download}]]
    |
        wait 50 ms
        [download[cancel {manager: @mgr2}]]
    ]
    -> @r where status is "cancelled" count > 0      ;; -> true
]
